<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns:ns1="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#" xmlns:ns2="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#" xmlns:ns3="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#" xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" />
    <ns1:label>Artefact</ns1:label>
    <ns1:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Object" />
    <ns2:definition>An object that is designed and made by humans for serving certain purposes or functions</ns2:definition>
    <ns2:example>A vehicle, a hammer, an appliance, a duct, a glass, a house, a cloth, an electronic device, a fork, a software, an artwork</ns2:example>
    <ns2:note>Artefact can be either schematic or concrete: a software - in abstraction from its installated copies - is an artefact; a software's installed copies are also artefacts; a symphony - as a work of music, in abstraction from its performances - is also an artefact</ns2:note>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" />
    <ns1:label>Information Artefact</ns1:label>
    <ns1:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Artefact" />
    <ns2:definition>An artefact that consists of a structured system of symbols' occurrences meant to convey information</ns2:definition>
    <ns2:example>A book, an article, a note, a document, a digital file, a computer programme or software</ns2:example>
    <ns2:note>An information artefact can be either a schematic object or a concrete object: War and Peace is schematic, whereas its copies are concrete; a PDF file is schematic, whereas its copies are concrete; a software is schematic, whereas its installed copies are concrete</ns2:note>
    <ns2:note>An information artefact that is concrete is always going to be the concretization of some schematic information artefact</ns2:note>
    <ns2:note>The information carried by an information artefact does not need to be intelligible for humans: for example, certain files or codes carry informaton that is only meant to be executed by a machine</ns2:note>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/Text">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class" />
    <ns1:label>Text</ns1:label>
    <ns1:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact" />
    <ns2:definition>An information artefact that consists of sentences in some human-readable language and is meant to convey meaning to humans</ns2:definition>
    <ns2:example>A book, an article, a physical document, a text or document file, a book chapter, a dish recipe</ns2:example>
    <ns2:note>According to the definition, not only plain text files, but also document files are instances of loze:Text</ns2:note>
    <ns2:note>In a software system, a machine code might not be a text, because it's not always meant to convey meaning to humans; on the other hand, the source code is a text, since it's human-readable</ns2:note>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/artefacts-information-and-instructions">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology" />
    <ns1:label>Artefacts, Information, and Instructions</ns1:label>
    <ns3:imports rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/" />
    <ns2:definition>An extension of loze.ttl with classes and predicates related to artefacts, information artefacts, and instructions</ns2:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/hasInstruction">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty" />
    <ns1:domain rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality" />
    <ns1:label>has Instruction</ns1:label>
    <ns1:range rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact" />
    <ns3:inverseOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/isInstructionIn" />
    <ns2:definition>The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and an information artefact i if and only if i is the instruction in e</ns2:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/hasRecipe">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty" />
    <ns1:domain rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality" />
    <ns1:label>has Recipe</ns1:label>
    <ns1:range rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact" />
    <ns3:inverseOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/isRecipeIn" />
    <ns2:definition>The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and an information artefact i if and only if i is the recipe in e</ns2:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/hasStandard">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty" />
    <ns1:domain rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality" />
    <ns1:label>has Standard</ns1:label>
    <ns1:range rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Particular" />
    <ns3:inverseOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/isStandardIn" />
    <ns2:definition>The relation that obtains between an eventuality e and a particular p if and only if p is the standard in e</ns2:definition>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/isInstructionIn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty" />
    <ns1:domain rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact" />
    <ns1:label>is Instruction In</ns1:label>
    <ns1:range rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality" />
    <ns1:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/isStandardIn" />
    <ns2:definition>The sub-relation of loze:isStandardIn that obtains when the standard in question is an information artefact consisting of instructions for performing the eventuality</ns2:definition>
    <ns2:example>The assembly manual is the instruction in the assembly of a piece of furniture, the cookbook recipe is the instruction in the preparation of a cake, the workflow documentation is the instruction in the execution of a technical process, a computer programme or software is the instruction in a computational process or programme execution</ns2:example>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/isRecipeIn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty" />
    <ns1:domain rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/InformationArtefact" />
    <ns1:label>is Recipe In</ns1:label>
    <ns1:range rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality" />
    <ns1:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/isInstructionIn" />
    <ns2:definition>The sub-relation of loze:isInstructionIn that obtains when the instructions in question prescribes the ingredient ratio and/or doses and the procedure for producing concrete particulars of a certain type or with certain features</ns2:definition>
    <ns2:example>The cookbook doses and instructions are the recipe in the preparation of a cake, a pharmaceutical formulation is the recipe in the production of paracetamol tablets, the alloy formula togethet with the alloying instructions are the recipe in the manufacturing of steel slabs</ns2:example>
    <ns2:note>Whenever an eventuality has a recipe (loze:isRecipeIn) among its participants, it will also have a product (loze:isProductIn), which will be a concrete particular</ns2:note>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://loze-ontology.org/isStandardIn">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty" />
    <ns1:domain rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Particular" />
    <ns1:label>is Standard In</ns1:label>
    <ns1:range rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/Eventuality" />
    <ns1:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://loze-ontology.org/isTopicIn" />
    <ns2:definition>The sub-relation of loze:isTopicIn that obtains when the topic provides the rules or standard for the performance of the eventuality</ns2:definition>
    <ns2:example>Regulations are the standard in a compliant business operation, best practices are the standard in a technical development, the workflow is the standard in the execution of a technical process</ns2:example>
    <ns2:note>Only eventualities that have humans as agents (loze:isAgentIn) or are executed by objects designed by humans - like an automated system - can have a standard (loze:isStandardIn), since treating something as a rule or standard for an action requires human-like cognitive faculties</ns2:note>
  </rdf:Description>
</rdf:RDF>